---
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const title = "Why the Morning Report Became a Tracker - Harness Dreams";
const description =
  "A design note on why Harness Dreams moved from a morning report ritual to a tracker-first MVP.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="article-page">
      <header class="article-hero">
        <div class="container section-narrow">
          <span class="eyebrow">Blog / Design note</span>
          <h1>Why the morning report became a tracker.</h1>
          <p class="lead">
            The MVP no longer asks testers to read a ceremony. It gives them a
            living Summary and lets advice appear only when the data earns it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="container article-grid">
        <aside class="article-aside card">
          <img
            src="/story-daily-report.png"
            alt=""
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            class="aside-image"
          />
          <span class="mono">Principle</span>
          <strong>Open with the tracker.</strong>
          <p>Advice supports the score. It does not replace the habit.</p>
        </aside>

        <div class="article-body">
          <p>
            Developers do not need another morning dashboard. They need a clear
            state they can recognize throughout the day: score, Code Flow, counters,
            trends, highlights, and the occasional grounded note.
          </p>

          <h2>Lead with the Dream Score</h2>
          <p>
            The Dream Score is the front door because it is quick, comparable,
            and deterministic. A tester should know whether today is Lucid,
            Dreaming, Restless, or Turbulent before reading a single card.
          </p>

          <h2>Keep evidence close</h2>
          <p>
            Every trend, highlight, quote, and coach card should open back to
            visible evidence: the component, code-flow moment, metric detail, or
            quote that caused it. Advice without an anchor is dashboard noise.
          </p>

          <h2>Make silence acceptable</h2>
          <p>
            A good day may have no advice. The app should be comfortable saying
            “No notes. Great day.” That restraint is what lets the rare coach
            card feel worth reading.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</SiteLayout>
